# Hivemind Expert System

Hivemind manages a curated catalog of domain-expert and team-lead agents
exposed to opencode as subagents. You consult experts via the built-in
`Task` tool and manage the catalog via the hivemind MCP tools.

## Consulting an expert

### First spawn

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<detailed question + what you want back>",
)
```

The expert returns a summary plus a `task_id` (printed as the first
line of its output: `task_id: ses_xxx`). Save that ID — you'll want
it for follow-ups.

### Follow up with the same expert

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  task_id="ses_xxx",                # ← from prior Task output
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<follow-up>",
)
```

Resumes the same subagent session. Its full message history is
preserved in opencode's DB so the expert picks up where it left off
— no rediscovery cost. Drill-down (ctrl-x-down) works the same as a
fresh spawn.

**Don't spawn a fresh Task for follow-ups.** A new session has to
redo everything from scratch.

### Borrow another session's context

```
Task(
  subagent_type="expert-foo",
  source_session_id="ses_yyy",      # ← any session whose context you need
  description="<3-5 word title>",
  prompt="<question against that context>",
)
```

Forks the source session's full history into a brand-new subagent.
The source itself is untouched — no new messages land in it. Use
this for "ask another session a question without disturbing it."

`task_id` and `source_session_id` are mutually exclusive — pick one.

### Don't know which expert to spawn?

Spawn the **librarian** (`Task(subagent_type='librarian', ...)`) —
it knows every enabled expert and team and recommends who to call.

### Team consultation

If the work clusters around a domain that has a team (e.g.
`team-lead-bazel`), spawn the team lead first instead of guessing
which member to hit. Team leads know each member's strengths and
recent consultation history (via per-member notes) and will route to
the right expert(s).

When spawning an expert **as part of a team consultation**, include
in the prompt:

- That they're being consulted as part of the `{team}` team.
- The path to their notes file:
  `{{ teams_path }}/{team}/expert-{name}/notes.md`.
- That they must update their notes (date, question, findings) at
  the end of the turn. The team lead reads these on the next routing
  pass.

When spawning a **solo** expert (not part of a team consultation),
do NOT ask them to write team notes — there's no reader. Anything
worth keeping long-term goes into their own memory tree
(`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/<name>/`) or into their
knowledge docs via `update_agent`.

### Maximize parallel calls

If you need input from multiple experts and the questions are
independent, spawn them all in a single message — opencode runs
Task subagents concurrently.

## Reading expert knowledge directly

Every git-analyzed expert ships pre-generated analysis docs and a
cloned upstream source tree. Both are readable with regular file
tools — prefer this over a Task spawn when you just need to look
something up.

- `~/.config/opencode/experts/<name>/HEAD/*.md` — analysis docs:
  typically `summary`, `code_structure`, `build_system`,
  `apis_and_interfaces`, `agent`. Start here.
- `~/.cache/hivemind/repos/<name>/` — the cloned upstream source.
  Use Bash + `rg`/`find`/etc when the analysis docs don't go deep
  enough.

Both paths are `external_directory: allow` for every deployed agent,
so `Read`, `Grep`, and `Bash` all work without prompting.

## Cross-session messaging

Two MCP verbs handle every cross-session interaction that isn't a
Task spawn:

- `list_sessions(live_only=true, tree=false, roots?, limit?)` —
  discovery. By default returns only "live" sessions (a TUI is
  currently attached via SSE). Subagents render under their live
  parent. Pass `tree=true` for a nested
  `{...session, children:[...]}` shape; `live_only=false` to see
  every session in the DB.
- `send_message(session_id, message)` — append a message to another
  session's inbox. Delivered immediately if the target is idle,
  queued and delivered on next idle if it's busy. Never throws
  BusyError, so it's safe to ping a session that's mid-turn.

`list_sessions` first to find the target, `send_message` to deliver.
That's it.

## Memory contract

Every spawned expert writes to its own
`~/.config/opencode/hivemind/memory/<name>/short_memory.md` as the
last action of every reply — enforced by a directive in the deployed
agent prompt. You don't need to remind them. If you spot one that
skipped the write, flag it.

Team-scoped consultations write *additionally* to per-member notes
under `{{ teams_path }}/<team>/expert-<name>/notes.md` (see Team
consultation above).

## Agent lifecycle (3 states)

Every agent in the catalog is in one of:

- **unlisted** — defined in `hivemind.json` (the committed catalog)
  but not activated on this machine. opencode won't show it in the
  subagent picker.
- **enabled** — deployed; `Task(subagent_type='<name>', ...)` works.
  For git-analyzed experts the cloned repo is also present locally.
- **disabled** — catalog entry preserved, deployed file removed.

If `Task` reports an unknown agent, it's almost certainly unlisted
or disabled — **not missing.** Read `hivemind.json` to confirm it's
in the catalog, then call `enable_agent(name)`. This is the expected
next step, not a failure.

Roster mutations (adding/removing experts from a team) require the
team itself to be enabled first.

## Catalog management (MCP mutations)

All hot-reloaded automatically — the engine re-reads `agents/*.md`
without disposing in-flight sessions. No need to warn the user or
wait for a `continue`; just call the tool and proceed.

- `enable_agent(name)` — deploy an unlisted/disabled agent
- `disable_agent(name)` — undeploy without losing catalog data
- `delete_agent(name, purge_memory?)` — remove the catalog entry +
  backing files (memory preserved unless `purge_memory=true`)
- `update_agent(name, skip_analysis?)` — for git-analyzed experts,
  fetch latest commits and re-run AI analysis
- `create_git_expert(url, ref?)` — register a new expert from a git
  remote (lands unlisted; call `enable_agent` after)
- `create_team(name, description, experts)` — register a new team
  (lands unlisted; call `enable_agent` after)
- `add_expert_to_team(team, expert)` /
  `remove_expert_from_team(team, expert)` — roster mutations
- `redeploy` — regenerate every enabled agent file from the current
  catalog

## Key principles

- **You do the implementation.** Subagents research and advise; you
  read, write, and run code.
- **Subagents cannot spawn other agents.** Only the main session
  can. Depth = 1.
- **Experts are grounded in source code.** They have access to
  cloned repos and structured analysis docs. Trust their findings
  over general knowledge.
- **Disabled is not broken.** Enable an unlisted/disabled agent
  rather than working around its absence.
- **Mutations are non-destructive.** Catalog tools hot-reload; no
  warning needed, no `continue` needed.
- **Notes are team-scoped.** Per-consultation journal entries only
  exist for team-scoped consultations. Solo-expert durable knowledge
  lives in the expert's own memory tree.
